1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,069
A team wrote a hook: a program that Git
runs by itself at a fixed point of a

2
00:00:09,109 --> 00:00:14,041
command. It works. Stage a private key,
run git commit to save a snapshot, and the

3
00:00:14,081 --> 00:00:17,619
commit is refused. And yet a
key is in the history of main.

4
00:00:17,959 --> 00:00:22,329
How did it get there? Several ways,
and none of them needs bad intent.

5
00:00:22,369 --> 00:00:27,620
The commit was made by a rebase or a revert,
which on Git 2.55 don't run pre-commit.

6
00:00:27,660 --> 00:00:32,911
Or the author deleted the file from disk
and committed again, and a hook that checks

7
00:00:32,951 --> 00:00:37,951
the disk instead of the index was satisfied.
Or the author's clone never had the

8
00:00:37,991 --> 00:00:42,108
hook installed. Or the author typed
one extra option: --no-verify.

9
00:00:42,448 --> 00:00:47,234
Before you can answer "whose fault", you
need to know exactly which hooks run for

10
00:00:47,274 --> 00:00:51,226
which command, in which order, and
which of them can stop anything.

11
00:00:51,266 --> 00:00:54,502
That's today. The answer for
the CTO is the next video.

12
00:00:54,542 --> 00:00:56,171
Hold on to that word, index.

13
00:00:58,011 --> 00:01:01,881
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

14
00:01:01,921 --> 00:01:07,009
Hooks belong to the local side of the line
this chapter keeps drawing: the hooks

15
00:01:07,049 --> 00:01:11,688
folder inside the dot git folder never
travels with clone, fetch or push.

16
00:01:11,728 --> 00:01:16,688
You've used the things hooks attach to:
commit, merge, rebase, checkout, push.

17
00:01:16,728 --> 00:01:20,470
Today you watch Git call a
program at each of those points.

18
00:01:20,810 --> 00:01:24,677
First the tour, with one tracing
script installed under many names.

19
00:01:24,717 --> 00:01:29,458
Then the worked hooks: a pre-commit that
checks the index, Git's list of what goes

20
00:01:29,498 --> 00:01:34,356
into the next commit. A commit-msg that
enforces a convention, a pre-push guard, two

21
00:01:34,396 --> 00:01:39,021
informational hooks, and a pre-rebase that
refuses to rewrite published commits.

22
00:01:40,861 --> 00:01:46,136
name the hooks that run for a commit, a
checkout, a merge, a rebase and a push, in

23
00:01:46,176 --> 00:01:46,525
order;

24
00:01:46,865 --> 00:01:50,409
say which hooks can stop an
operation and which only observe;

25
00:01:50,749 --> 00:01:54,624
write a pre-commit hook that checks
the index and not the working tree;

26
00:01:54,964 --> 00:01:57,380
write a commit-msg hook
and a pre-push guard;

27
00:01:57,720 --> 00:02:01,050
explain why a hook that depends
on tools must check for them.

28
00:02:02,890 --> 00:02:07,758
In one sentence: a hook is an executable
file with a fixed name in the hooks

29
00:02:07,798 --> 00:02:13,118
directory, or, since Git 2.54, a command
named in configuration, that Git runs at a

30
00:02:13,158 --> 00:02:18,284
defined point of a command, passing
information as arguments and standard input.

31
00:02:18,324 --> 00:02:21,642
For some hooks, a non-zero
exit status means "stop".

32
00:02:21,682 --> 00:02:26,550
The exit status is the number a program
returns when it ends, and zero means

33
00:02:26,590 --> 00:02:27,067
success.

34
00:02:27,407 --> 00:02:30,895
Now precisely. The hooks
manual lists 28 hook names.

35
00:02:30,935 --> 00:02:36,459
Git looks for them in $GIT_DIR/hooks, or
in the directory named by core.hooksPath,

36
00:02:36,499 --> 00:02:42,090
and ignores files without the executable
bit. A hook runs at the top of the working

37
00:02:42,130 --> 00:02:47,722
tree, the directory of files you edit, with
GIT_DIR and related variables exported.

38
00:02:47,762 --> 00:02:51,861
git init copies inactive samples
from the template directory.

39
00:02:55,201 --> 00:02:59,551
For each hook, four facts matter: which
command runs it, what it receives as

40
00:02:59,591 --> 00:03:04,404
arguments and on standard input, whether a
non-zero exit stops the command, and what

41
00:03:04,444 --> 00:03:04,924
skips it.

42
00:03:05,264 --> 00:03:10,327
On the server side, git receive-pack runs
pre-receive once per push before any ref

43
00:03:10,367 --> 00:03:15,554
is updated, update once per pushed ref, and
post-receive and post-update afterwards.

44
00:03:15,594 --> 00:03:20,159
A ref is a name such as a branch.
Nothing on the client skips those hooks.

45
00:03:20,199 --> 00:03:25,013
An all-zero object ID in these inputs
means "does not exist": the ref is being

46
00:03:25,053 --> 00:03:28,374
created, on the old side, or
deleted, on the new side.

47
00:03:28,714 --> 00:03:30,793
Three design rules for hooks you write.

48
00:03:31,133 --> 00:03:36,874
Rule one. A commit is made from the index,
so a pre-commit hook reads the index, not

49
00:03:36,914 --> 00:03:42,586
the files on disk. That means git diff
--cached for content, and git cat-file -s on

50
00:03:42,626 --> 00:03:44,375
the staged path for sizes.

51
00:03:44,715 --> 00:03:47,838
Rule two. A "pre" hook that
can stop should be fast.

52
00:03:47,878 --> 00:03:52,948
The textbook's sentence is: use client-side
hooks for fast feedback to the person at

53
00:03:52,988 --> 00:03:56,658
the keyboard, and keep them fast,
or people learn the bypass.

54
00:03:56,998 --> 00:04:00,821
Rule three. A hook is a program
on a machine you don't control.

55
00:04:00,861 --> 00:04:05,911
If it needs a tool, it must check that the
tool is there, and decide what a missing

56
00:04:05,951 --> 00:04:09,038
tool means. The same care
goes for its exit status.

57
00:04:09,078 --> 00:04:14,127
A post-checkout hook can't undo a switch,
but its exit status becomes the status of

58
00:04:14,167 --> 00:04:18,235
the command, so a failing hook
breaks scripts that test git switch.

59
00:04:18,575 --> 00:04:23,102
And when should you not rely on a hook?
Whenever the check must hold for everyone.

60
00:04:23,142 --> 00:04:27,501
A hook that "checks every commit" checks
the commits made by git commit and git

61
00:04:27,541 --> 00:04:29,951
merge. That's the subject
of the next video.

62
00:04:31,791 --> 00:04:35,171
The textbook's analogy:
tripwires in your own workshop.

63
00:04:35,211 --> 00:04:38,778
You place them. They do nothing
in a colleague's workshop.

64
00:04:38,818 --> 00:04:40,457
And you can step over them.

65
00:04:40,797 --> 00:04:45,390
The analogy fits client-side hooks. It breaks
for server-side ones, which sit at the

66
00:04:45,430 --> 00:04:47,321
door that every delivery must pass.

67
00:04:47,661 --> 00:04:50,619
Keep the two kinds apart
for the rest of the course.

68
00:04:50,659 --> 00:04:54,136
A tripwire gives fast feedback
to the person in the workshop.

69
00:04:54,176 --> 00:04:57,999
A check at the door is the only
thing that inspects every delivery.

70
00:04:59,839 --> 00:05:04,919
On the first line, three gates can stop a
commit and --no-verify opens two of them.

71
00:05:04,959 --> 00:05:09,793
On the second line, the vertical bar is
the boundary between your clone and the

72
00:05:09,833 --> 00:05:13,309
other repository. Everything
left of it is yours to skip.

73
00:05:13,349 --> 00:05:15,098
Everything right of it isn't.

74
00:05:16,938 --> 00:05:20,219
Samples, all with the suffix
.sample, all inactive.

75
00:05:20,259 --> 00:05:25,689
Now one tracing script is installed under
ten client-side names and four server-side

76
00:05:25,729 --> 00:05:26,080
names.

77
00:05:26,420 --> 00:05:31,893
It prints its own name, its arguments and,
for some hooks, its standard input, and

78
00:05:31,933 --> 00:05:37,473
exits with 0. Your first prediction: how
many hooks does a plain git commit -m run,

79
00:05:37,513 --> 00:05:40,633
and in which order? Say
it out loud. I'll wait.

80
00:05:45,473 --> 00:05:50,080
Four, in this order: pre-commit,
prepare-commit-msg, commit-msg, post-commit.

81
00:05:50,120 --> 00:05:55,150
prepare-commit-msg received the message file
and the word "message": it learned that

82
00:05:55,190 --> 00:05:56,658
the message came from -m.

83
00:05:56,998 --> 00:06:01,402
The same four, and then post-rewrite with
the argument amend, and on standard input

84
00:06:01,442 --> 00:06:03,222
the old commit ID and the new one.

85
00:06:03,562 --> 00:06:08,119
Two IDs, because an amend writes a new
commit that takes the place of the old one.

86
00:06:08,459 --> 00:06:13,575
post-checkout receives the previous HEAD,
meaning where you were, the new HEAD and a

87
00:06:13,615 --> 00:06:18,056
flag. Creating a branch doesn't change
the commit, so both IDs are equal.

88
00:06:18,096 --> 00:06:22,168
Restoring a file from a commit is
a "file checkout": the flag is 0.

89
00:06:22,508 --> 00:06:26,844
A merge runs pre-merge-commit in place of
pre-commit, and post-merge at the end.

90
00:06:27,184 --> 00:06:32,211
Now a rebase that replays one commit,
which means it makes a copy with a new ID.

91
00:06:32,251 --> 00:06:35,822
Do pre-commit and commit-msg
run for the replayed commit?

92
00:06:35,862 --> 00:06:37,025
Predict. I'll wait.

93
00:06:41,865 --> 00:06:44,712
They don't. pre-rebase ran
once and post-rewrite once.

94
00:06:45,052 --> 00:06:48,959
For the replayed commit,
prepare-commit-msg and post-commit ran.

95
00:06:48,999 --> 00:06:53,707
pre-commit and commit-msg didn't. If you
expected them to run, that's normal.

96
00:06:53,747 --> 00:06:58,824
The textbook adds that git revert, which
adds a commit that undoes another, behaves

97
00:06:58,864 --> 00:07:03,202
the same on Git 2.55. This is the
first answer to our opening question.

98
00:07:03,542 --> 00:07:08,259
pre-push ran in your clone, with the remote
name and URL as arguments and one line

99
00:07:08,299 --> 00:07:13,074
for the ref on standard input. The remote
ID is all zeros because the branch is new

100
00:07:13,114 --> 00:07:13,422
there.

101
00:07:13,762 --> 00:07:18,385
The lines that start with "remote:" were
printed by hooks in the other repository

102
00:07:18,425 --> 00:07:21,953
and sent back: pre-receive,
update, post-receive, post-update.

103
00:07:22,293 --> 00:07:27,669
Try it now, on paper, thirty seconds.
Write the four commit hooks in order, then

104
00:07:27,709 --> 00:07:30,987
cross out the ones you
predict --no-verify skips.

105
00:07:35,827 --> 00:07:40,049
Check your paper. --no-verify
skipped pre-commit and commit-msg.

106
00:07:40,389 --> 00:07:46,441
prepare-commit-msg and post-commit still
ran. Of these two, prepare-commit-msg can

107
00:07:46,481 --> 00:07:51,567
still stop a commit by exiting
non-zero, with or without --no-verify.

108
00:07:51,607 --> 00:07:55,356
The textbook confirmed that
by a run on Git 2.55.0.

109
00:07:55,696 --> 00:08:00,837
This hook looks at what is staged: git diff
--cached for a private-key header, and

110
00:08:00,877 --> 00:08:05,892
the size of each staged file. Now a deploy
key and a 600 kilobyte checkpoint are

111
00:08:05,932 --> 00:08:08,103
staged together with a real change.

112
00:08:11,443 --> 00:08:15,053
Two messages on standard error,
exit status 1, no commit.

113
00:08:15,093 --> 00:08:19,280
Now the developer deletes the two
files from disk and tries again.

114
00:08:19,320 --> 00:08:23,571
Quick quiz: does the commit go
through now, or is it refused again?

115
00:08:23,611 --> 00:08:24,595
Say it out loud.

116
00:08:29,435 --> 00:08:34,049
Refused again. The hook reads the
index, and the files are still staged.

117
00:08:34,089 --> 00:08:37,863
Only after git restore --staged
does the commit go through.

118
00:08:38,203 --> 00:08:41,699
On disk, deploy_key is gone. In
the index it's still staged.

119
00:08:41,739 --> 00:08:46,059
A hook that had looked at the disk
would have let the key into the commit.

120
00:08:46,099 --> 00:08:51,009
That's the index, paid off. Know the limits
of this hook, though: file names without

121
00:08:51,049 --> 00:08:54,485
spaces only, one secret pattern,
and one option removes it.

122
00:08:54,825 --> 00:08:57,470
Exit status 0. The key is committed.

123
00:08:57,810 --> 00:09:01,221
Refused, with the expected
form and the subject it got.

124
00:09:01,261 --> 00:09:04,797
Then look at git status -s:
the change shows as unstaged.

125
00:09:04,837 --> 00:09:09,941
git commit -a stages into a temporary index
and discards it when the commit fails.

126
00:09:09,981 --> 00:09:13,203
The lab also shows the
classic defect of such hooks.

127
00:09:13,243 --> 00:09:18,409
They refuse the subjects that Git writes
itself, "Merge branch" and "fixup!", and a

128
00:09:18,449 --> 00:09:20,040
merge then stops half-way.

129
00:09:20,380 --> 00:09:24,884
The push is refused before anything is
sent, and the hook names the unfinished

130
00:09:24,924 --> 00:09:26,747
commit. main is still two ahead.

131
00:09:27,087 --> 00:09:31,507
The hook uses its three arguments: it
exits at once for a file checkout, and

132
00:09:31,547 --> 00:09:34,793
compares the lock file between
the old and the new HEAD.

133
00:09:40,333 --> 00:09:45,624
The pull was a fast-forward, with no merge
commit, and post-merge ran all the same.

134
00:09:45,664 --> 00:09:46,908
Now the exit status.

135
00:09:47,248 --> 00:09:51,783
The hook exited with 3, git switch exited
with 1, and the branch was switched.

136
00:09:51,823 --> 00:09:56,006
Think of a hook that calls a tool
which isn't installed on this machine.

137
00:09:56,046 --> 00:10:00,933
The command "fails", the switch has happened,
and every script that tests the status

138
00:10:00,973 --> 00:10:05,743
of git switch breaks. That's why a hook
that depends on tools must check for them,

139
00:10:05,783 --> 00:10:08,499
and exit 0 with a message
when they're missing.

140
00:10:08,839 --> 00:10:13,304
It counts the commits to be rebased, and
the ones among them that no remote-tracking

141
00:10:13,344 --> 00:10:14,162
branch contains.

142
00:10:14,502 --> 00:10:18,101
A remote-tracking branch records
where a branch was on the server.

143
00:10:18,141 --> 00:10:20,417
If the numbers differ, some are published.

144
00:10:24,957 --> 00:10:29,850
The local branch was rebased. The pushed
one wasn't: "The pre-rebase hook refused to

145
00:10:29,890 --> 00:10:31,494
rebase." And the usual door:

146
00:10:31,834 --> 00:10:35,704
Rebased. The branch is now 2 ahead
of and 1 behind its upstream.

147
00:10:36,044 --> 00:10:39,714
Here it is as a picture: the state
the rebase module warned you about.

148
00:10:41,554 --> 00:10:43,202
Five mistakes to watch for.

149
00:10:43,542 --> 00:10:48,514
A pre-commit hook that inspects files on
disk. Root cause: a commit is made from the

150
00:10:48,554 --> 00:10:52,869
index; what is staged can differ from
what is on disk in both directions.

151
00:10:53,209 --> 00:10:57,209
Believing a pre-commit or commit-msg
hook checks every commit.

152
00:10:57,249 --> 00:11:02,226
Root cause: on Git 2.55 the commits
replayed by a rebase, and those made by a

153
00:11:02,266 --> 00:11:03,529
revert, run neither.

154
00:11:03,869 --> 00:11:06,975
A commit-msg pattern that
rejects Git's own subjects.

155
00:11:07,015 --> 00:11:11,843
Root cause: merges and fixup commits have
generated subjects, and the hook stops a

156
00:11:11,883 --> 00:11:12,733
merge half-way.

157
00:11:13,073 --> 00:11:16,732
A post-checkout hook that exits
non-zero when a tool is missing.

158
00:11:16,772 --> 00:11:21,588
Root cause: its exit status becomes the
status of git switch although the switch has

159
00:11:21,628 --> 00:11:22,108
happened.

160
00:11:22,448 --> 00:11:27,298
A slow "pre" hook. Root cause: it runs
at every commit for the person at the

161
00:11:27,338 --> 00:11:31,545
keyboard; people learn --no-verify,
and then no check runs at all.

162
00:11:33,385 --> 00:11:37,922
Now, out of the lab. An ML platform
team installs four hooks in its service

163
00:11:37,962 --> 00:11:42,987
repositories. A pre-commit that checks
staged content for key headers and for files

164
00:11:43,027 --> 00:11:47,625
above a size limit, with a message that
points to Git LFS or the data store.

165
00:11:47,665 --> 00:11:52,629
A commit-msg hook for the subject convention,
which lets through the subjects that

166
00:11:52,669 --> 00:11:53,728
Git writes itself.

167
00:11:54,068 --> 00:11:58,878
A post-checkout and post-merge pair that
says when the dependency lock file changed,

168
00:11:58,918 --> 00:12:02,516
and that exits 0 with a note when
the comparison can't be made.

169
00:12:02,556 --> 00:12:07,077
And a pre-push guard that keeps commits
whose subject starts with WIP off main.

170
00:12:07,417 --> 00:12:10,569
Every one of them gives
feedback in under a second.

171
00:12:10,609 --> 00:12:14,761
None of them is described in the
team's documentation as a control.

172
00:12:14,801 --> 00:12:20,018
The team knows which commits bypass them
and that one option skips them, so the same

173
00:12:20,058 --> 00:12:24,774
checks run again where every push arrives.
That placement is the next video.

174
00:12:26,614 --> 00:12:30,873
Your turn. Do Lab 14.2,
"A commit-msg hook", in

175
00:12:30,913 --> 00:12:34,715
lab-manual/m14-hooks-rerere-attributes.md.

176
00:12:35,055 --> 00:12:39,648
Before each commit in the lab, predict
whether the hook accepts the message, and

177
00:12:39,688 --> 00:12:44,340
what git status -s shows after a refusal.
Before you merge and before you revert,

178
00:12:44,380 --> 00:12:48,684
predict which hooks run. The lab's
failure scenario stops a merge half-way.

179
00:12:48,724 --> 00:12:51,638
Predict the state of the
repository at that moment.

180
00:12:51,978 --> 00:12:56,363
The challenge is Lab 14.3, "A
pre-push guard", in the same file.

181
00:13:00,203 --> 00:13:05,278
Answer out loud. A strong answer gives four
names in order and says for each whether

182
00:13:05,318 --> 00:13:10,393
it can stop the commit. It divides them by
the --no-verify flag, and notices the one

183
00:13:10,433 --> 00:13:13,865
hook that can still stop a
commit when the flag is given.

184
00:13:13,905 --> 00:13:18,919
For the second half it names commands, says
which hooks they do run, and is careful

185
00:13:18,959 --> 00:13:23,547
about the version. Then it draws the
conclusion the question aims at: what a

186
00:13:23,587 --> 00:13:26,166
client-side hook can
and can't be used for.

187
00:13:28,006 --> 00:13:32,458
Let's land this. One picture to
keep: gone from disk, still staged.

188
00:13:32,798 --> 00:13:34,458
You should now be able to say:

189
00:13:34,798 --> 00:13:39,644
A hook is a program Git runs at a fixed
point with defined arguments and standard

190
00:13:39,684 --> 00:13:43,083
input; a non-zero exit from
some hooks stops the command.

191
00:13:43,423 --> 00:13:49,534
git commit runs pre-commit, prepare-commit-msg,
commit-msg, post-commit; --no-verify

192
00:13:49,574 --> 00:13:51,730
skips the first and the third.

193
00:13:52,070 --> 00:13:57,512
A rebase and a revert create commits without
running pre-commit or commit-msg on Git

194
00:13:57,552 --> 00:13:57,838
2.55.

195
00:13:58,178 --> 00:14:02,595
A pre-commit hook must read the index;
a pre-push hook reads one line per ref.

196
00:14:02,935 --> 00:14:07,286
post-checkout and post-merge observe,
and a hook that needs a tool must behave

197
00:14:07,326 --> 00:14:08,411
sensibly without it.

198
00:14:10,251 --> 00:14:17,372
Read sections 14C.9 and 14C.10 of Chapter
14C. Do Exercise 14.3, Level 1, "A

199
00:14:17,412 --> 00:14:24,910
pre-commit hook, and its limit", and
Exercise 14.6, Level 2, command prediction,

200
00:14:24,950 --> 00:14:31,129
"Which hooks run", in
exercises/m11-m15-investigation-recovery.md.

201
00:14:31,469 --> 00:14:36,600
Today you watched Git call a program at
every gate, and predicted which gates can

202
00:14:36,640 --> 00:14:41,579
stop. Practise with the labs first. Next
time: why hooks can't enforce policy.

203
00:14:41,619 --> 00:14:44,898
Until then, look at the
state first and type second.

204
00:14:44,938 --> 00:14:46,430
See you in the next one.
